__d(function(e,a,t,o,i,r,s){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"English",textbook:"Honeysuckle (Supplementary)",chapter_number:14,chapter_title:"Water",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the central theme of the poem 'Water'?",back:"The central theme is that water is the most precious gift of nature and the source of all life. The poem celebrates water's role in sustaining humans, animals, plants, and the earth, and urges every reader to conserve it and never waste a single drop.",memory_hook:"WATER = Without A Thing, Everything Rots! That is the poem's entire message in one acronym."},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name three living beings that depend on water according to the poem's theme.",back:"Three living beings that depend on water are: (1) Humans \u2014 who need water for drinking, cooking, bathing, and farming. (2) Animals and birds \u2014 who drink from rivers, ponds, and lakes. (3) Plants and crops \u2014 which need rain and irrigation to grow and produce food.",memory_hook:"H-A-P: Humans, Animals, Plants \u2014 all need water to be HAPpy and alive!"},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does the word 'precious' mean? Why is water called precious in the poem?",back:"'Precious' means of great value \u2014 something rare, irreplaceable, and not to be wasted. Water is called precious because all life depends on it and clean, fresh water is available in very limited quantities on Earth. Less than 1% of all water on Earth is fresh and easily accessible.",memory_hook:"Precious = Price-less + Essential. A diamond is precious because it is rare. Water is MORE precious because without it, even a diamond is useless!"},{id:"fc04",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does land look like when there is no water?",back:"Without water, land becomes barren \u2014 dry, cracked, bare, and unable to grow any crops or support any plants. Animals and people leave or perish. The land looks brown and lifeless, like a desert. This is why the poem compares the absence of water to death itself.",memory_hook:"No water = No green = No life. Picture a dry, cracked mud field in peak summer \u2014 THAT is what 'barren' means. Now picture it after rain: total transformation!"},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does 'conserve' mean? Give one example of conserving water at school.",back:"'Conserve' means to save something carefully and use it wisely so it does not run out. One example of conserving water at school: turning off the tap completely after washing hands rather than leaving it dripping, which can save hundreds of litres of water each day across the whole school.",memory_hook:"CONserve = CONtrol your use. Think of a 'con artist' who takes too much \u2014 you must NOT be that person with water. Control, save, protect!"},{id:"fc06",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is 'imagery' in a poem? Give one example of water imagery.",back:"Imagery is when a poet uses vivid, descriptive language to create strong mental pictures that appeal to the senses (sight, sound, smell, touch, taste). An example of water imagery: 'The river gushed silver and cold over smooth pebbles' \u2014 this creates a visual picture and almost lets you hear and feel the rushing water.",memory_hook:"IMAGE-ry = it creates an IMAGE in your mind. If you can close your eyes and SEE, HEAR, or FEEL what the poem describes \u2014 that is imagery at work!"},{id:"fc07",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is 'personification'? Give an example using water.",back:"Personification is a literary device in which non-human things are given human qualities, feelings, or actions. Example using water: 'The river danced joyfully over the rocks' \u2014 rivers cannot literally dance, but giving them this human action makes the description more vivid and emotionally engaging.",memory_hook:"Person-ification = making a NON-person act like a PERSON. River dances, rain laughs, ocean roars \u2014 these are all personification!"},{id:"fc08",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is clean freshwater so rare and precious on Earth?",back:"About 97.5% of Earth's water is salty ocean water that humans cannot drink. Of the remaining 2.5% fresh water, most is locked in glaciers and ice caps. Less than 1% is accessible in rivers, lakes, and underground wells. This tiny fraction must support 8 billion people, all animals, and all agriculture \u2014 making fresh water one of the rarest and most precious resources on the planet.",memory_hook:"97.5% = Salty (can't drink). 2.5% = Fresh but mostly frozen. Less than 1% = What ALL of us actually use. That less-than-1% is what the poem is protecting!"},{id:"fc09",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a 'drought'? How does it connect to the poem's message?",back:"A drought is a prolonged period with very little or no rainfall, causing severe water shortages, crop failures, and suffering for both people and animals. It directly connects to the poem's message: when water is wasted or polluted, and when rainfall fails, drought-like conditions emerge. The poem warns that water is not infinite \u2014 careless use and climate change can lead to drought, making conservation an urgent duty.",memory_hook:"DROUGHT rhymes with 'out' \u2014 when rain is 'out' (absent), drought comes in. No rain + No conservation = Drought crisis. The poem says: PREVENT it by saving water NOW."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does 'replenish' mean? How does rain replenish water sources?",back:"'Replenish' means to fill something up again after it has been used or emptied. Rain replenishes water sources by falling from clouds, soaking into the ground to refill underground wells (groundwater), flowing into rivers and lakes, and filling tanks and reservoirs. Without regular rain, these sources slowly empty and cannot support life.",memory_hook:"Re-PLENISH = fill again until PLENTIFUL. Think of a PLEN (plenty) of water being poured back in. Rain is nature's way of hitting the 'refill' button!"},{id:"fc11",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does leaving a tap running for one minute while brushing teeth waste water? Why does it matter?",back:"A running tap wastes approximately 6 litres of water per minute. A school of 500 students doing this twice a day wastes 6,000 litres daily \u2014 enough drinking water for an entire village. Multiplied across a city or country, billions of litres are wasted annually. It matters because that wasted water could have served communities in drought-prone areas like Kolar or Bellary in Karnataka, where people walk kilometres for water.",memory_hook:"1 minute tap = 6 litres gone. 500 students \xd7 2 times = 6,000 litres wasted daily. The tap drips \u2014 the village suffers. TURN IT OFF!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does the Cauvery river dispute in Karnataka connect to the poem's message about water?",back:"The Cauvery river water dispute between Karnataka and Tamil Nadu has lasted decades because both states depend on the river's limited water for farming and drinking. It is a real-world example of what happens when water is scarce \u2014 communities and even states come into conflict. The poem's message \u2014 water is precious, conserve it, never waste it \u2014 is the exact lesson that would prevent such conflicts if practised by everyone.",memory_hook:"Cauvery = C for Conflict caused by scarcity. The poem teaches: if we all conserve, the conflict over water shrinks. SAVE water = SAVE peace!"},{id:"fc13",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name two poetic devices used in the poem 'Water' and give one example of each.",back:"1. Imagery: 'The river gushed silver and cold over the pebbles' \u2014 creates a visual and sensory picture of flowing water. 2. Personification: 'The rain called out to the parched earth, promising life' \u2014 rain is given the human ability to call and make promises, making the description more emotionally powerful and vivid.",memory_hook:"Imagery = PICTURE in words. Personification = PERSON in a non-person. Together they make the poem feel ALIVE \u2014 you don't just read about water, you EXPERIENCE it!"},{id:"fc14",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why does the poem urge us to protect rivers and ponds from pollution?",back:"Rivers and ponds are vital freshwater sources for drinking, farming, and animal life. When they are polluted with plastic, sewage, chemicals, or garbage, the water becomes unsafe to drink and harmful to aquatic life. Once a water body is severely polluted, cleaning it is extremely expensive and time-consuming. The poem urges protection because preventing pollution is far easier than reversing its damage \u2014 and the lives of millions of living beings depend on clean water bodies.",memory_hook:"Pollute a river = Kill a lifeline. Think of the river as a blood vessel \u2014 if you poison it, the whole body (ecosystem) gets sick. PROTECT = PREVENT pollution before it starts."},{id:"fc15",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does the Jal Jeevan Mission in Karnataka connect to the poem's theme?",back:"The Jal Jeevan Mission (also called Har Ghar Jal \u2014 'water for every home') is a Government of India scheme that aims to provide clean piped drinking water to every rural household by 2024. In Karnataka, this scheme targets villages in dry districts like Kolar, Chitradurga, and Raichur where water access has historically been difficult. This scheme is a real-life implementation of the poem's message: water is a right for all living beings, not a luxury \u2014 and every effort must be made to ensure clean water reaches everyone.",memory_hook:"Jal Jeevan = 'Water is Life' in Hindi \u2014 exactly the poem's title and theme! The government scheme and the poem share the SAME three words as their core message."},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Design a one-week water conservation plan for your home based on the poem's message.",back:"A one-week home water conservation plan: Monday \u2014 turn off tap while brushing (saves 6L/use). Tuesday \u2014 report any leaking taps to parents. Wednesday \u2014 reuse vegetable-washing water to water garden plants. Thursday \u2014 take shorter showers (save up to 50L). Friday \u2014 collect rainwater in a bucket for cleaning purposes. Saturday \u2014 check all taps are fully closed before sleeping. Sunday \u2014 teach one family member one water-saving habit. Over 7 days, this plan can save hundreds of litres and build lifelong conservation habits.",memory_hook:"7 days, 7 habits, 700 litres saved. The poem asks for ACTION \u2014 this plan IS the poem put into daily life!"},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Use imagery to write two sentences about rain falling on a dry field, connecting to the poem's message.",back:"Sample answer: 'The first drops of monsoon rain hammered the cracked, thirsty earth like tiny silver fists, and within minutes the air filled with the sweet smell of wet soil awakening from its long drought. What had been a brown, silent wasteland trembled to life \u2014 blades of grass pushing through the softened ground, as if the earth itself had been holding its breath, waiting for water to give it permission to live again.'",memory_hook:"Good imagery = reader SEES, SMELLS, FEELS. Did your two sentences use at least two senses? Sight + Smell + Touch = powerful imagery. PRACTICE makes perfect!"},{id:"fc18",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does 'barren' mean? How does the poem contrast barren land with fertile land?",back:"'Barren' means dry, infertile, and unable to support plant or animal life. The poem contrasts barren land (without water \u2014 cracked, brown, empty, lifeless) with fertile land (with water \u2014 green, productive, full of crops and living things). This contrast is the poem's central visual argument: water is what separates life from death in the natural world. The contrast also motivates the reader emotionally \u2014 who would want their land to be barren when conservation can keep it fertile?",memory_hook:"BARREN = BARE + ROTTEN prospects. FERTILE = FULL + THRIVING life. Water is the switch between them. Conserve water = Stay on the FERTILE side!"},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How can a Class 6 student in Karnataka become a 'Water Ambassador' at school, inspired by the poem?",back:"A Class 6 student can become a Water Ambassador by: (1) Forming a small group of classmates who pledge to conserve water daily. (2) Making posters with lines from the poem and putting them near school taps as reminders. (3) Reporting leaking pipes and taps to the headmaster. (4) Organising a school assembly presentation on water conservation using facts from the poem. (5) Starting a rainwater harvesting project in the school garden. These actions transform the poem's message from words on a page into real community impact.",memory_hook:"AMBASSADOR = A person who represents and spreads a message. YOU can be water's ambassador. The poem gives you the message \u2014 your actions make it real!"},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"In your own words, explain why the poem 'Water' is still relevant in today's world. Give two real-world reasons.",back:"The poem 'Water' is deeply relevant today for two key reasons: (1) Global water crisis \u2014 over 2 billion people worldwide lack access to safe drinking water, and climate change is making droughts and floods more frequent. The poem's call to conserve water addresses this crisis directly. (2) Local relevance in Karnataka \u2014 cities like Bengaluru face severe water shortage every summer, and districts like Kolar depend on water tankers for daily needs. The poem's message is not historical \u2014 it is happening right now, all around us, making its moral urgently and personally relevant to every student in Karnataka.",memory_hook:"RELEVANT = Real + Important + Happening NOW. Water scarcity is not a future problem \u2014 it is today's problem. The poem is a TODAY poem, not a history poem!"}]}},2952,[]);